1
00:00:02,367 --> 00:00:03,825
HELLY: <i>I acknowledge that, henceforth,</i>

2
00:00:03,909 --> 00:00:08,205
<i>my access to my memories
will be spatially dictated.</i>

3
00:00:09,291 --> 00:00:11,792
<i>I will be unable to access
outside recollections</i>

4
00:00:11,876 --> 00:00:14,921
<i>whilst on Lumon's severed basement floor,</i>

5
00:00:15,005 --> 00:00:17,424
<i>nor retain work memories upon my ascent.</i>

6
00:00:19,801 --> 00:00:22,970
I am aware that this alteration

7
00:00:23,054 --> 00:00:25,599
is comprehensive and irreversible.

8
00:00:27,642 --> 00:00:29,192
I make these statements freely.

9
00:00:30,729 --> 00:00:34,189
MR. MILCHICK: Okay, that's a wrap.

10
00:00:37,276 --> 00:00:38,326
Come with me, please.

11
00:00:42,489 --> 00:00:43,824
[CHUCKLES]

12
00:00:44,992 --> 00:00:48,328
So your work personage will be waking
in a few hours on the severed floor.

13
00:00:48,412 --> 00:00:51,665
But the next time you yourself
will be sentient will be this evening,

14
00:00:51,749 --> 00:00:53,208
in the elevator back up.

15
00:00:53,292 --> 00:00:54,293
Okay.

16
00:00:54,835 --> 00:00:58,338
The elevator has a handrail
at hip-level if you feel dizzy.

17
00:00:58,422 --> 00:00:59,840
Oh, man.

18
00:01:02,509 --> 00:01:04,887
I love seeing the sunrise on his face.

19
00:01:06,555 --> 00:01:09,432
You know he used to drink three
raw eggs in milk each morning?

20
00:01:09,516 --> 00:01:10,517
I've heard.

21
00:01:10,601 --> 00:01:12,601
-[CHUCKLES] His favorite breakfast.
-Mmm.

22
00:01:13,062 --> 00:01:14,605
We'll get you prepped in here.

23
00:01:15,648 --> 00:01:17,548
-Good morning, Lawrence.
-Good morning.

24
00:01:25,115 --> 00:01:29,912
[MONITOR BEEPING]

25
00:01:43,925 --> 00:01:45,260
They say it doesn't hurt.

26
00:01:50,139 --> 00:01:51,474
MR. MILCHICK: Just relax.

27
00:01:52,976 --> 00:01:54,978
[BEEPING CONTINUES]

28
00:02:04,946 --> 00:02:06,364
[DRILL WHIRS]

29
00:02:07,740 --> 00:02:09,909
MR. MILCHICK: Little vibration now.

30
00:02:09,993 --> 00:02:11,494
[DRILL WHIRRING]

31
00:02:47,572 --> 00:02:50,073
I'll be upstairs after to
let you know how it went.

32
00:02:52,034 --> 00:02:53,884
Sorry if I freak out on you a little.

33
00:02:55,579 --> 00:02:58,498
Don't worry. I'm very excited to meet you.

34
00:03:07,174 --> 00:03:09,176
[BEEPING CONTINUES]

35
00:03:24,650 --> 00:03:27,193
[HEARTBEAT THUMPING]

36
00:03:27,277 --> 00:03:29,071
[BEEPING DISTORTS]

37
00:03:35,202 --> 00:03:36,411
[DOOR OPENS]

38
00:03:40,791 --> 00:03:41,875
Hey, Helly.

39
00:03:42,709 --> 00:03:44,002
Whoa, what's happening?

40
00:03:44,086 --> 00:03:45,212
Great to see you.

41
00:03:46,380 --> 00:03:48,180
Your orientation's been so much fun.

42
00:03:49,591 --> 00:03:52,261
Uh, where am I?

43
00:03:52,803 --> 00:03:57,472
Okay, so sometimes when a new
hire is adjusting to a severed space,

44
00:03:57,556 --> 00:03:59,766
we help by bringing
them here to the stairwell,

45
00:03:59,850 --> 00:04:01,727
to experience the transition viscerally.

46
00:04:01,811 --> 00:04:03,687
Oh, no. I'm trying to leave?

47
00:04:03,771 --> 00:04:04,980
No, no.

48
00:04:05,064 --> 00:04:06,523
It's all part of the process.

49
00:04:06,607 --> 00:04:09,707
If you wanna spin around and
head back in, that should be that.

50
00:04:13,906 --> 00:04:14,907
Hey, there.

51
00:04:17,826 --> 00:04:20,496
-What the hell?
-[CHUCKLES] That's okay.

52
00:04:21,330 --> 00:04:22,915
Not a problem.

53
00:04:22,999 --> 00:04:24,708
Wanna give it another shot?

54
00:04:24,792 --> 00:04:26,210
Maybe with a little oomph?

55
00:04:28,546 --> 00:04:29,589
[BUZZING]

56
00:04:31,549 --> 00:04:32,883
Hi.

57
00:04:32,967 --> 00:04:34,259
I'm at the stairwell.

58
00:04:34,343 --> 00:04:36,846
[INHALES DEEPLY] It's going fair.

59
00:04:38,097 --> 00:04:40,349
-No, she's…
-[SHOUTS] Ah, fuck!

60
00:04:40,433 --> 00:04:43,060
Ooh. Nah, I'ma call you back.

61
00:04:45,313 --> 00:04:47,523
Wow. [CHUCKLES]

62
00:04:47,607 --> 00:04:50,484
-[EXHALES DEEPLY]
-You're an inquisitive one.

63
00:04:50,568 --> 00:04:52,569
[SIGHS] I don't wanna be in there, do I?

64
00:04:52,653 --> 00:04:54,321
You're learning that you do.

65
00:04:54,405 --> 00:04:55,405
-[SIGHS]
-Hey,

66
00:04:55,489 --> 00:04:58,951
when we heard you were
coming here, it was like a miracle.

67
00:04:59,619 --> 00:05:01,662
It's amazing what you're doing.

68
00:05:11,338 --> 00:05:12,755
[ELEVATOR BELL DINGS]

69
00:05:12,839 --> 00:05:14,674
[HIGH-PITCHED RINGING]

70
00:05:14,758 --> 00:05:15,842
[ELEVATOR BELL DINGS]

71
00:05:24,351 --> 00:05:25,852
MR. MILCHICK: Hey, old-timer!

72
00:05:25,936 --> 00:05:27,228
One day down.

73
00:06:48,726 --> 00:06:50,561
[FOOTSTEPS APPROACHING]

74
00:08:42,296 --> 00:08:43,297
HELLY: Hey.

75
00:08:44,297 --> 00:08:45,298
Hey.

76
00:08:45,382 --> 00:08:46,675
So it's tomorrow now?

77
00:08:47,342 --> 00:08:48,594
Uh, yeah.

78
00:08:49,094 --> 00:08:50,887
Well, it's Monday.

79
00:08:51,680 --> 00:08:53,264
-A weekend just happened?
-Yeah.

80
00:08:53,348 --> 00:08:54,766
I don't even feel like I left.

81
00:08:54,850 --> 00:08:57,561
Yeah, that's how nights
and weekends feel here.

82
00:08:58,353 --> 00:08:59,354
Like nothing?

83
00:08:59,438 --> 00:09:00,563
Well, you get used to it.

84
00:09:00,647 --> 00:09:03,274
I mean, I find it helps to
focus on the effects of sleep

85
00:09:03,358 --> 00:09:05,693
since we don't actually
get to experience it.

86
00:09:05,777 --> 00:09:09,280
You may feel rejuvenated or happy.

87
00:09:09,364 --> 00:09:11,950
Less tense in the shoulders. Spry.

88
00:09:14,286 --> 00:09:15,995
So it's 09:05?

89
00:09:16,079 --> 00:09:19,332
Yeah, they stagger the entries
too so we don't meet on the outside.

90
00:09:19,416 --> 00:09:20,751
It's important apparently.

91
00:09:23,378 --> 00:09:25,005
So I guess we're not friends.

92
00:09:26,798 --> 00:09:27,966
Guess not.

93
00:09:32,137 --> 00:09:34,137
DYLAN: My current
file's called "Tumwater…"

94
00:09:34,221 --> 00:09:36,431
Which I started some 11 weeks back.

95
00:09:36,515 --> 00:09:37,850
"Tumwater." All one word.

96
00:09:37,934 --> 00:09:39,476
Should I be taking notes?

97
00:09:39,560 --> 00:09:41,895
-MARK: No.
-DYLAN: I've got it 96% sorted,

98
00:09:41,979 --> 00:09:44,648
which means I've earned
four of the five tier incentives,

99
00:09:44,732 --> 00:09:49,320
including the erasers and the finger
traps that you see displayed here.

100
00:09:49,904 --> 00:09:53,074
100% is tier five.
That gets you a caricature portrait.

101
00:09:53,866 --> 00:09:57,035
You'll note I've accrued an
embarrassment of wealth in that regard.

102
00:09:57,119 --> 00:09:58,579
-Wow.
-DYLAN: Correct.

103
00:09:58,663 --> 00:10:01,040
Each one of these?
Finished file in the can.

104
00:10:01,749 --> 00:10:03,375
So that's the highest award?

105
00:10:03,459 --> 00:10:04,585
DYLAN: Percentage-wise, yes.

106
00:10:04,669 --> 00:10:06,753
But if we hit our
numbers by quarter's end,

107
00:10:06,837 --> 00:10:08,881
one of us gets named
refiner of the quarter,

108
00:10:08,965 --> 00:10:11,258
and that shit gets you a waffle party.

109
00:10:11,342 --> 00:10:12,926
I'm sorry, a waffle party?

110
00:10:13,010 --> 00:10:14,428
Okay, hazards on, eager lemur.

111
00:10:14,512 --> 00:10:18,012
I'm a dead lock for that this quarter,
so, uh, don't get your hopes up.

112
00:10:20,685 --> 00:10:22,485
What about Mark's crystal head cube?

113
00:10:23,062 --> 00:10:25,481
That's not a prize, that's
just something they gave him.

114
00:10:25,565 --> 00:10:27,942
MARK: Helly, could
you flip on this console?

115
00:10:35,324 --> 00:10:36,450
[SWITCH CLICKS]

116
00:10:38,953 --> 00:10:40,912
[BEEPING]

117
00:10:41,830 --> 00:10:43,623
[ELECTRONIC SONG PLAYS]

118
00:10:44,207 --> 00:10:45,542
MARK: Okay.

119
00:10:48,128 --> 00:10:50,130
-That's… Excuse me. [TYPING]
-Mm-hmm.

120
00:10:56,469 --> 00:10:59,013
Mark, sorry to interrupt.
I know you're training Helly.

121
00:10:59,097 --> 00:11:02,308
I just noticed you've removed
the group photos from the desks.

122
00:11:02,392 --> 00:11:05,520
Yeah. We're gonna take the
new ones at Helly's party today.

123
00:11:06,771 --> 00:11:07,772
Oh.

124
00:11:08,732 --> 00:11:11,943
Okay, this is the Siena file.

125
00:11:12,027 --> 00:11:13,861
Now, all the data you see

126
00:11:13,945 --> 00:11:17,406
falls into one of four
essential categories.

127
00:11:17,490 --> 00:11:19,617
And we group each line of code,

128
00:11:19,701 --> 00:11:24,247
and then sort it evenly
between five digital buckets.

129
00:11:24,331 --> 00:11:25,331
HELLY: Party.

130
00:11:25,415 --> 00:11:27,365
Just poke around first. Use the arrows.

131
00:11:27,918 --> 00:11:31,295
I do think the old photos are
supposed to stay on the desk

132
00:11:31,379 --> 00:11:33,006
until the new ones come in.

133
00:11:40,013 --> 00:11:41,014
Right.

134
00:11:41,598 --> 00:11:43,850
-HELLY: Should this mean something to me?
-No.

135
00:11:43,934 --> 00:11:46,434
No, all the data comes
from upstairs fully encoded.

136
00:11:47,479 --> 00:11:48,979
Then how do I categorize it?

137
00:11:51,065 --> 00:11:52,107
What?

138
00:11:53,567 --> 00:11:58,738
Each category of numbers
presents in such an order as to elicit

139
00:11:58,822 --> 00:12:01,241
an emotional response in the refiner.

140
00:12:01,325 --> 00:12:07,539
Um… So, uh, Cat 1 numbers, for
example, feel a certain way on sight.

141
00:12:07,623 --> 00:12:10,792
They'll be sort of disconcerting, scary.

142
00:12:10,876 --> 00:12:11,877
Scary?

143
00:12:11,961 --> 00:12:13,170
I know.

144
00:12:13,254 --> 00:12:15,005
My job is to scroll
through this spreadsheet

145
00:12:15,089 --> 00:12:16,673
and look for numbers that are scary?

146
00:12:16,757 --> 00:12:18,759
It sounds dumb, and Mark said it dumb.

147
00:12:19,343 --> 00:12:22,012
-[SIGHS]
-Are the numbers bloody? Do they chant?

148
00:12:22,096 --> 00:12:24,973
It doesn't make sense till you
see it, and it takes a while to see.

149
00:12:25,057 --> 00:12:26,933
Hey, Mark. I just printed out

150
00:12:27,017 --> 00:12:29,394
the passage of the handbook
on changing out group photos.

151
00:12:29,478 --> 00:12:32,606
Just might be good to peruse
when you're between things.

152
00:12:32,690 --> 00:12:34,525
-MARK: Thank you.
-Sorry.

153
00:12:37,945 --> 00:12:39,029
Am I trapped here?

154
00:12:41,240 --> 00:12:42,699
In what way?

155
00:12:42,783 --> 00:12:46,495
Like if it turns out I hate this and
I want to quit, is that an option?

156
00:12:48,622 --> 00:12:49,707
[MARK SIGHS]

157
00:12:50,207 --> 00:12:51,250
Look.

158
00:12:52,585 --> 00:12:53,877
If you're really unhappy,

159
00:12:53,961 --> 00:12:58,172
you can submit a resignation
request with your outside self for review.

160
00:12:58,256 --> 00:13:00,132
DYLAN: And good luck
getting that approved.

161
00:13:00,216 --> 00:13:03,928
Well, they do tend to be rejected.
Plus, you know…

162
00:13:05,513 --> 00:13:06,514
What?

163
00:13:07,390 --> 00:13:12,228
Well, since this perceptual
version of you only exists at Lumon,

164
00:13:13,062 --> 00:13:15,523
I mean, quitting would
effectively end your life.

165
00:13:15,607 --> 00:13:17,807
I mean, in so much as
you've come to know it.

166
00:13:19,569 --> 00:13:20,820
MR. MILCHICK: Hello, Refiners.

167
00:13:20,904 --> 00:13:23,197
Ooh, sweet. Melon bar.

168
00:13:23,281 --> 00:13:24,740
Hi, Mr. Milchick.

169
00:13:24,824 --> 00:13:27,159
Helly, welcome.

170
00:13:27,243 --> 00:13:30,747
I'm agog at how well I can
tell you're already fitting in.

171
00:13:31,789 --> 00:13:33,458
The office feels whole.

172
00:13:34,876 --> 00:13:38,587
Now, let's get this party started.

173
00:13:38,671 --> 00:13:39,672
[CHUCKLES]

174
00:13:44,594 --> 00:13:47,930
Well, uh, my name is
Irving, as you all know.

175
00:13:48,014 --> 00:13:50,182
And I've worked here for three years,

176
00:13:50,266 --> 00:13:55,062
and something about me is that I
know all nine core Lumon principles.

177
00:13:55,146 --> 00:13:57,607
Awesome. What's your favorite?

178
00:14:00,610 --> 00:14:01,611
All nine.

179
00:14:02,486 --> 00:14:06,739
But today, I think I'd say, "cheer."

180
00:14:06,823 --> 00:14:07,824
Great.

181
00:14:11,203 --> 00:14:14,289
Uh-oh! Uh, no trust fall today, Irv.

182
00:14:14,873 --> 00:14:17,501
IRVING: Oh. Right.

183
00:14:26,218 --> 00:14:28,262
No. No.

184
00:14:35,978 --> 00:14:37,937
Hello, I'm Helly.

185
00:14:38,021 --> 00:14:39,731
I've been at Lumon for, like…

186
00:14:40,732 --> 00:14:41,900
Ten hours total, and…

187
00:14:44,486 --> 00:14:46,736
I'm sorry, I don't know
anything about myself.

188
00:14:47,614 --> 00:14:48,907
Oh, sure you do, Helly.

189
00:14:50,325 --> 00:14:51,368
I really don't.

190
00:14:52,744 --> 00:14:53,953
I guess I went home last night,

191
00:14:54,037 --> 00:14:56,122
but I don't know if home is
a house, or an apartment,

192
00:14:56,206 --> 00:14:57,290
or if I live with a family…

193
00:14:57,374 --> 00:15:00,043
I like to think my outie
lives on, like, a riverboat.

194
00:15:02,170 --> 00:15:03,671
I'm sorry, outies are…

195
00:15:03,755 --> 00:15:06,549
They're us on the outside.

196
00:15:06,633 --> 00:15:08,843
You saw yours yesterday in the video.

197
00:15:08,927 --> 00:15:12,596
Right. I actually have a few things to
say to her. Can I record something back?

198
00:15:12,680 --> 00:15:14,223
[IRVING CHUCKLES]

199
00:15:14,307 --> 00:15:16,975
So what you'll find here is that
communication between selves

200
00:15:17,059 --> 00:15:18,436
is pretty curtailed.

201
00:15:18,936 --> 00:15:20,521
So what if I write her a note?

202
00:15:20,605 --> 00:15:24,650
Fortunately, the elevators are equipped
with something called "code detectors."

203
00:15:25,359 --> 00:15:27,027
So messages can't be passed through.

204
00:15:27,111 --> 00:15:30,531
Yeah… They're like metal
detectors for written symbols.

205
00:15:30,615 --> 00:15:31,865
A Lumon original, apparently.

206
00:15:31,949 --> 00:15:33,033
That's right. Right.

207
00:15:33,117 --> 00:15:34,952
Sure. Okay, well what if, uh, I…

208
00:15:35,036 --> 00:15:37,786
I don't think you're quite
getting the game here, Helly.

209
00:15:38,915 --> 00:15:39,957
May I?

210
00:15:42,835 --> 00:15:44,086
Guys, this is Helly.

211
00:15:44,170 --> 00:15:47,506
She's 30 years old, she's allergic
to almonds and has weak enamel.

212
00:15:48,257 --> 00:15:51,301
At 5'6", she's the fourth
tallest person in your office,

213
00:15:51,385 --> 00:15:54,555
and her hair is what
we call shoulder-length.

214
00:15:56,182 --> 00:16:00,478
And seeing her here with you all, I'd
say she most definitely has a family.

215
00:16:01,312 --> 00:16:02,563
Aw.

216
00:16:05,149 --> 00:16:09,153
Oh, wow. Well, that's a lot to follow.

217
00:16:09,237 --> 00:16:12,573
Uh, so I'll just say that I'm Mark.

218
00:16:12,657 --> 00:16:18,370
Been with Lumon about two years,
and I absolutely love this game.

219
00:16:19,871 --> 00:16:23,625
Uh, nice try, pal, but
you said that last time.

220
00:16:24,960 --> 00:16:26,627
[GROANS] Fair enough.

221
00:16:26,711 --> 00:16:28,713
Uh… [CHUCKLES] Um…

222
00:16:29,631 --> 00:16:31,633
[CLEARS THROAT] Well, I, um…

223
00:16:34,719 --> 00:16:35,845
[GULPS]

224
00:16:36,846 --> 00:16:40,392
I broke protocol this morning.

225
00:16:43,979 --> 00:16:46,981
I was dusting the old group
photos, the ones with Petey,

226
00:16:47,065 --> 00:16:48,608
and it just…

227
00:16:49,901 --> 00:16:52,112
Made me feel sad.

228
00:16:53,697 --> 00:16:55,323
And, I guess,

229
00:16:56,283 --> 00:17:00,161
worried that I won't be
able to run MDR like he did.

230
00:17:00,245 --> 00:17:02,289
That tracks. I have similar worries.

231
00:17:03,623 --> 00:17:04,833
So I…

232
00:17:05,834 --> 00:17:08,919
Took 'em from the cubicles
and put 'em in the storage closet,

233
00:17:09,003 --> 00:17:11,214
which we're not supposed to do.

234
00:17:13,592 --> 00:17:15,760
I recall this. I objected.

235
00:17:19,513 --> 00:17:21,182
Thank you for telling me, Mark.

236
00:17:22,725 --> 00:17:25,310
I actually find your reaction sweet.

237
00:17:25,394 --> 00:17:29,439
Though, it is puzzling you have
an outburst like this for Petey,

238
00:17:29,523 --> 00:17:32,610
and not for, say, Carol D.

239
00:17:33,569 --> 00:17:36,613
But we knew Carol D.
was leaving beforehand.

240
00:17:36,697 --> 00:17:38,657
I mean, her outie filmed a thank-you.

241
00:17:39,617 --> 00:17:41,785
Petey was just gone.

242
00:17:43,162 --> 00:17:45,163
And I mean, I… I don't know

243
00:17:45,247 --> 00:17:51,419
if he's at some new job or
drunk on a beach, or dead…

244
00:17:51,503 --> 00:17:53,303
MR. MILCHICK: That's enough, please.

245
00:18:02,973 --> 00:18:05,100
I think this is a good
time to remind ourselves

246
00:18:05,184 --> 00:18:08,312
that things like deaths
happen outside of here.

247
00:18:09,772 --> 00:18:10,773
Not here.

248
00:18:12,191 --> 00:18:15,236
A life at Lumon is
protected from such things.

249
00:18:16,362 --> 00:18:22,034
And I think a great potential response
to that from all of you is gratitude.

250
00:18:30,167 --> 00:18:33,670
I also think that melon
isn't getting any tastier.

251
00:18:36,381 --> 00:18:38,716
["THE CAT" PLAYING]

252
00:19:08,788 --> 00:19:10,456
Hey, sorry I derailed your game.

253
00:19:11,499 --> 00:19:15,336
I thought I already had, but
then, yeah, you made it way worse.

254
00:19:15,420 --> 00:19:16,421
[CHUCKLES]

255
00:19:18,548 --> 00:19:20,841
So how are you gonna
figure out if Petey's okay?

256
00:19:20,925 --> 00:19:23,428
Oh, I think Milchick was pretty clear.

257
00:19:24,345 --> 00:19:26,096
You're just done asking
about your best friend

258
00:19:26,180 --> 00:19:28,180
because our babysitter told you to stop?

259
00:19:30,101 --> 00:19:32,269
You know, Milchick's a nice man.

260
00:19:32,353 --> 00:19:35,231
When he says something,
it's best to listen.

261
00:19:35,315 --> 00:19:38,150
-I don't care.
-'Cause he can't always be nice like that.

262
00:19:38,234 --> 00:19:40,151
MR. MILCHICK: [CLAPS] Okay, Refiners!

263
00:19:40,235 --> 00:19:43,404
Let's get this new group photo
before the melon bloat sets in.

264
00:19:43,488 --> 00:19:44,698
[LAUGHS]

265
00:19:55,500 --> 00:19:56,543
IRVING: At last.

266
00:19:59,379 --> 00:20:00,880
-MR. MILCHICK: All right.
-IRVING: Mm-hmm.

267
00:20:00,964 --> 00:20:04,414
Great big smiles. Remember, you're
gonna be looking at this every day.

268
00:20:06,219 --> 00:20:07,679
Say "gratitude."

269
00:20:08,388 --> 00:20:09,938
IRVING, DYLAN, MARK: Gratitude!

270
00:20:12,809 --> 00:20:13,936
Say "cheer."

271
00:20:14,478 --> 00:20:15,938
IRVING, DYLAN, MARK: Cheer!

272
00:20:20,317 --> 00:20:22,235
Helly? What are you doing?

273
00:20:22,319 --> 00:20:26,197
Oh, I… I just think I'm not
gonna work here anymore. Sorry.

274
00:20:26,281 --> 00:20:27,449
What do you mean?

275
00:20:28,033 --> 00:20:28,992
I quit.

276
00:20:29,076 --> 00:20:30,785
I don't wanna do the file-sorting thing

277
00:20:30,869 --> 00:20:33,204
or the never-seeing-the-sun-thing
or the disappearing-friends thing.

278
00:20:33,288 --> 00:20:35,748
-I just don't want any of it.
-We told you there's code detectors.

279
00:20:35,832 --> 00:20:39,544
Do you know that? Have you tried?
Because frankly, it sounds made-up.

280
00:20:47,635 --> 00:20:48,636
MARK: Helly!

281
00:20:50,513 --> 00:20:52,097
[PANTS]

282
00:20:52,181 --> 00:20:53,307
MARK: Helly!

283
00:20:55,768 --> 00:20:56,852
[BEEPS]

284
00:21:00,314 --> 00:21:02,233
[ALARM BLARING]

285
00:21:04,944 --> 00:21:07,196
Please, you don't know…

286
00:21:09,115 --> 00:21:10,700
Oh, God. Come on.

287
00:21:15,788 --> 00:21:17,248
[HELLY GROANS, PANTS]

288
00:21:21,043 --> 00:21:23,170
Shit. Mr. Graner?

289
00:21:24,255 --> 00:21:25,590
[ALARM STOPS]

290
00:21:26,757 --> 00:21:27,758
Come on out.

291
00:21:28,843 --> 00:21:31,053
[HELLY PANTING]

292
00:21:33,681 --> 00:21:35,099
What have we got here?

293
00:21:38,227 --> 00:21:39,728
Perhaps you'd better come with me.

294
00:21:39,812 --> 00:21:41,212
MARK: [KNOCKING] Mr. Graner.

295
00:21:42,189 --> 00:21:43,524
[BEEPS]

296
00:21:43,608 --> 00:21:47,820
Hey. I see you've found my
wayward trainee. [PANTS]

297
00:21:48,946 --> 00:21:50,990
Appreciate your help, sir, as always.

298
00:21:54,034 --> 00:21:55,576
Ah, the note. [CHUCKLES]

299
00:21:55,660 --> 00:21:59,455
So, this is embarrassing, but,
uh, it's my first time training,

300
00:21:59,539 --> 00:22:03,752
and I must've just forgotten to go
over data-smuggling rules with Helly.

301
00:22:04,628 --> 00:22:06,754
Oh, sorry. This is Helly. [CHUCKLES]

302
00:22:06,838 --> 00:22:07,839
Helly, Mr. Graner.

303
00:22:07,923 --> 00:22:09,674
[INHALES SHAKILY] Yeah.

304
00:22:12,218 --> 00:22:14,720
Yeah, so if Helly tripped
the code detectors,

305
00:22:14,804 --> 00:22:19,351
that one, it's on me and,
uh, I apologize for that, sir.

306
00:22:19,851 --> 00:22:20,852
Earnestly.

307
00:22:22,646 --> 00:22:24,981
-Big department chief now, Mark S.?
-Huh?

308
00:22:25,065 --> 00:22:27,025
Well… [CHUCKLES]

309
00:22:27,609 --> 00:22:30,570
On you, then. Let's go.

310
00:23:06,647 --> 00:23:07,648
[BEEPS]

311
00:23:18,993 --> 00:23:20,093
-[BEEPS]
-[LOCK CLICKS]

312
00:23:46,687 --> 00:23:47,938
MS. COBEL: Mark.

313
00:23:51,108 --> 00:23:52,984
So you're a doula?

314
00:23:53,068 --> 00:23:55,404
-DATE: A midwife, actually.
-Hmm.

315
00:23:57,740 --> 00:24:01,577
And how many deliveries
have you, I don't know, seen?

316
00:24:02,661 --> 00:24:04,121
Oh, uh…

317
00:24:05,456 --> 00:24:06,874
Over 300?

318
00:24:07,499 --> 00:24:09,250
This is mostly back in Montana.

319
00:24:09,334 --> 00:24:11,794
-Wow. That's, uh, so cool.
-Yeah.

320
00:24:11,878 --> 00:24:14,213
That's such a high amount.

321
00:24:14,297 --> 00:24:17,800
[CHUCKLES] Yeah. We get, uh, two a day.

322
00:24:18,927 --> 00:24:19,969
-Depending.
-Hmm.

323
00:24:20,053 --> 00:24:21,346
-Mm-hmm.
-Mmm.

324
00:24:30,063 --> 00:24:32,773
So, is… is that, uh, like, ten a week?

325
00:24:32,857 --> 00:24:34,775
Like, in a five-day work week, or…

326
00:24:34,859 --> 00:24:36,486
-Yeah, or so.
-Yeah. Okay.

327
00:24:37,487 --> 00:24:41,157
-Are you vetting me for your sister?
-No. Oh, no. You seem great.

328
00:24:41,241 --> 00:24:42,784
-Yeah, I'm kidding.
-I know.

329
00:24:52,377 --> 00:24:53,460
-Thank you.
-WAITER: Mm-hmm.

330
00:24:53,544 --> 00:24:56,047
So, Lumon?

331
00:24:57,173 --> 00:24:59,008
-Yeah.
-Like half this town.

332
00:24:59,842 --> 00:25:01,886
And half of me. That was a joke.

333
00:25:01,970 --> 00:25:04,013
[CHUCKLES] It was funny.

334
00:25:04,097 --> 00:25:09,810
Yeah. Um, I'm in the archives division,
sort of a corporate historian, apparently.

335
00:25:09,894 --> 00:25:10,895
Mmm.

336
00:25:10,979 --> 00:25:14,941
Um, so a lot of sensitive material,
hence the… [CLICKS TONGUE]

337
00:25:16,942 --> 00:25:18,360
So you don't know…

338
00:25:19,820 --> 00:25:23,198
Who you work with, or what you do, or…
or anything?

339
00:25:23,282 --> 00:25:24,991
Yeah, that's the idea.

340
00:25:25,075 --> 00:25:27,675
-What if you snuck in a note?
-You can't sneak notes.

341
00:25:27,953 --> 00:25:28,996
So…

342
00:25:30,080 --> 00:25:31,956
-You could have a girlfriend at Lumon…
-Thank you.

343
00:25:32,040 --> 00:25:33,041
…and not know it.

344
00:25:33,125 --> 00:25:36,225
And if you met someone out
here, you wouldn't know it in there.

345
00:25:36,336 --> 00:25:38,213
Like, you could get married and have kids,

346
00:25:38,297 --> 00:25:40,840
and just forget they exist
for eight hours every day,

347
00:25:40,924 --> 00:25:42,301
for your whole life.

348
00:25:42,885 --> 00:25:44,535
That doesn't mess with your head?

349
00:25:47,681 --> 00:25:50,434
I think for some people, it's the point.

350
00:25:55,355 --> 00:25:59,609
DATE: So, as a local, this just feels
like a reasonable temperature to you?

351
00:25:59,693 --> 00:26:01,653
MARK: Well, technically, I'm from Ganz.

352
00:26:01,737 --> 00:26:04,072
And you're one to talk.

353
00:26:04,156 --> 00:26:08,368
Isn't Minnesota, like,
crazy cold with the lakes?

354
00:26:08,452 --> 00:26:10,787
Yes. Minnesota was very cold

355
00:26:10,871 --> 00:26:14,791
the one time I visited
from my home in Montana.

356
00:26:14,875 --> 00:26:17,085
Oh, so… so those are different places.

357
00:26:18,170 --> 00:26:19,296
Uh-huh.

358
00:26:20,464 --> 00:26:22,215
So do you live in Lumon housing?

359
00:26:23,466 --> 00:26:25,300
You make it sound like dorms or something.

360
00:26:25,384 --> 00:26:26,385
[BOTH CHUCKLE]

361
00:26:26,469 --> 00:26:28,637
-No, I just meant that there's…
-I live in Baird Creek.

362
00:26:28,721 --> 00:26:33,100
And, yes, they happen to be subsidized
by the company that employs me.

363
00:26:33,184 --> 00:26:35,769
PETITIONER 1: Excuse me, do you
have a moment for children's brain health?

364
00:26:35,853 --> 00:26:37,896
PETITIONER 2: And legalized
severance in the workplace?

365
00:26:37,980 --> 00:26:41,650
-Oh, nice. The WMC's out and about.
-Who?

366
00:26:41,734 --> 00:26:44,361
PETITIONER 1: Most severed workers
don't see the sun their whole lives.

367
00:26:44,445 --> 00:26:46,738
The Whole Mind Collective. They're great.

368
00:26:46,822 --> 00:26:49,449
Uh, excuse me, do you have a
moment for children's brain health?

369
00:26:49,533 --> 00:26:50,576
PASSERBY: We're all good.

370
00:26:50,660 --> 00:26:52,536
You folks have a moment
for children's brain health?

371
00:26:52,620 --> 00:26:53,495
-We do.
-Great!

372
00:26:53,579 --> 00:26:55,289
We're trying to get a
measure on the ballot

373
00:26:55,373 --> 00:26:58,125
to keep mega-corporations like
Lumon from continuing to force

374
00:26:58,209 --> 00:26:59,459
legalized severance on our state.

375
00:26:59,543 --> 00:27:00,919
They're forcing it now?

376
00:27:01,003 --> 00:27:03,797
That's what they're lobbying for.
And Jame Eagan is trying to sever kids…

377
00:27:03,881 --> 00:27:07,134
Okay, well, what about the
self-mutilating types who do it willingly?

378
00:27:07,218 --> 00:27:09,720
I mean, I heard that some
of them are so deluded

379
00:27:09,804 --> 00:27:11,555
they don't even know they're victims.

380
00:27:11,639 --> 00:27:14,224
I also heard that if you're severed,

381
00:27:14,308 --> 00:27:17,394
you go to two separate hells.
Is that true?

382
00:27:17,478 --> 00:27:19,897
Hey, man, you wanna benefit
off forced labor, that's up…

383
00:27:19,981 --> 00:27:22,191
-Hey, man. Forced labor?
-…then that's up to you.

384
00:27:22,275 --> 00:27:23,317
Fucking really?

385
00:27:23,401 --> 00:27:24,610
-Hey!
-Yeah.

386
00:27:24,694 --> 00:27:25,986
-Forced labor? Okay.
-Really.

387
00:27:26,070 --> 00:27:28,530
So people can just, like, self-imprison?

388
00:27:28,614 --> 00:27:30,115
Are you captive right now?

389
00:27:30,199 --> 00:27:34,410
No, seriously, because your past
self chose to walk you down here

390
00:27:34,494 --> 00:27:36,954
to be an infantilizing prick to people.

391
00:27:37,038 --> 00:27:39,832
Severance is subjugation, asshole.

392
00:27:39,916 --> 00:27:42,668
Oh, oh, that's nice language for a…

393
00:27:42,752 --> 00:27:44,170
What… What are you? Twelve?

394
00:27:44,254 --> 00:27:47,132
Are you 12 years old?
Are you even in high school yet?

395
00:27:49,801 --> 00:27:51,303
Okay. Uh, come on.

396
00:27:53,388 --> 00:27:55,223
-[REFRIGERATOR DOOR OPENS]
-[SIGHS]

397
00:28:08,320 --> 00:28:10,322
[SIGHS]

398
00:28:14,242 --> 00:28:15,368
[KNOCKS]

399
00:28:22,000 --> 00:28:23,417
MARK: Mrs. Selvig.

400
00:28:23,501 --> 00:28:24,794
Peace offering.

401
00:28:25,837 --> 00:28:26,838
Are we fighting?

402
00:28:26,922 --> 00:28:30,049
I just keep thinking
about those damn bins.

403
00:28:30,133 --> 00:28:35,429
Oh. Well, that is so not necessary,
but, uh, very kind. Thank you.

404
00:28:35,513 --> 00:28:37,432
[SIGHS, SHIVERS]

405
00:28:38,807 --> 00:28:41,268
Here, why don't you come in? I have milk.

406
00:28:41,977 --> 00:28:43,729
-Why, thank you!
-[CHUCKLES] Yeah.

407
00:28:45,564 --> 00:28:49,901
Oh, I'm experimenting with
chamomile, so no hard feelings if you gag.

408
00:28:49,985 --> 00:28:51,153
Oh, I'm sure I won't.

409
00:28:54,406 --> 00:28:58,076
Still waiting for that
third bulb to revive itself?

410
00:28:58,160 --> 00:29:01,329
Oh, yeah. Keep forgetting to change that.

411
00:29:01,413 --> 00:29:04,833
Hmm, my, you smell nice.
Were you on a date?

412
00:29:04,917 --> 00:29:06,918
Uh, sort of.

413
00:29:07,002 --> 00:29:12,799
My sister set me up with,
like, her doula, or midwife.

414
00:29:12,883 --> 00:29:14,552
Didn't really feel like anything.

415
00:29:19,932 --> 00:29:22,518
Well, let's see here. I'll, uh…

416
00:29:29,149 --> 00:29:30,233
Mmm.

417
00:29:30,317 --> 00:29:31,417
[MRS. SELVIG CHUCKLES]

418
00:29:32,111 --> 00:29:33,237
Wow.

419
00:29:33,737 --> 00:29:35,906
-This is… These are magic.
-Oh. [CHUCKLES]

420
00:29:35,990 --> 00:29:37,116
Mm-hmm.

421
00:29:37,908 --> 00:29:39,409
[SIGHS]

422
00:29:39,493 --> 00:29:41,328
My late husband was a carpenter,

423
00:29:41,412 --> 00:29:42,871
and before he passed,

424
00:29:42,955 --> 00:29:47,834
he said he would start building
us a house in the hereafter.

425
00:29:48,876 --> 00:29:52,338
And there would be a small
guest apartment in the back,

426
00:29:52,422 --> 00:29:55,383
in case I found a new
man before I got there.

427
00:29:59,012 --> 00:30:00,889
That's… so sweet.

428
00:30:01,723 --> 00:30:03,891
[LAUGHS] Yes.

429
00:30:03,975 --> 00:30:07,562
He even drew blueprints,
which I keep in my purse.

430
00:30:08,438 --> 00:30:09,647
[CHUCKLES]

431
00:30:09,731 --> 00:30:12,108
[LAUGHS, SIGHS]

432
00:30:13,109 --> 00:30:14,693
Well, come by the shop, okay?

433
00:30:14,777 --> 00:30:19,365
I'll give you a mugwort bath bomb
that'll make you sleep like a rag doll.

434
00:30:19,449 --> 00:30:20,909
I'll try to get down there.

435
00:30:21,993 --> 00:30:23,494
Good night, Mrs. Selvig.

436
00:30:30,084 --> 00:30:31,085
[SIGHS]

437
00:31:19,967 --> 00:31:21,968
PERSON 1 ON TV: <i>The nerve,
my new partner's a woman.</i>

438
00:31:22,052 --> 00:31:24,304
PERSON 2: <i>I just think you
could've mentioned it sooner.</i>

439
00:31:24,388 --> 00:31:28,808
PERSON 3: <i>You'd better be on
the stakeout and not… out for a steak.</i>

440
00:31:28,892 --> 00:31:30,519
[AUDIENCE LAUGHING]

441
00:31:45,951 --> 00:31:47,202
[SIGHING]

442
00:31:59,756 --> 00:32:01,507
[SIGHS]

443
00:32:08,806 --> 00:32:10,391
[SIGHS, GRUNTS]

444
00:32:26,115 --> 00:32:27,909
[LINE RINGING]

445
00:32:28,784 --> 00:32:30,911
MR. MILCHICK: <i>Seth
Milchick, Lumon Industries.</i>

446
00:32:30,995 --> 00:32:33,080
Mr. Milchick, it's Mark Scout.

447
00:32:33,164 --> 00:32:36,124
I'm a severed worker.
Employee number 4502.

448
00:32:36,208 --> 00:32:39,211
<i>Mr. Scout, what a pleasure.
How can I help you?</i>

449
00:32:39,295 --> 00:32:41,755
I actually woke up
not feeling terribly well.

450
00:32:41,839 --> 00:32:43,424
I'm sorry I'm just now calling.

451
00:32:44,091 --> 00:32:47,219
<i>Oh, no. May I ask how serious it is?</i>

452
00:32:47,303 --> 00:32:51,974
Well, nothing bad, just
very minor stomach event.

453
00:32:52,058 --> 00:32:53,351
Should be back tomorrow.

454
00:32:54,435 --> 00:32:55,561
<i>Well, I'm sorry.</i>

455
00:32:55,645 --> 00:32:58,272
<i>I know your innie will be
sad to have missed the day.</i>

456
00:33:00,566 --> 00:33:03,027
<i>You feel better. Okay, Mr. Scout?</i>

457
00:33:07,989 --> 00:33:10,366
-Where's Mark?
-Missed you too.

458
00:33:10,450 --> 00:33:13,369
He should be coming down first now.
You don't think that…

459
00:33:13,453 --> 00:33:16,038
He's probably sick. They wouldn't
bounce him and Petey the same week.

460
00:33:16,122 --> 00:33:17,456
I certainly hope not.

461
00:33:17,540 --> 00:33:18,958
Quarterly deadline's coming up,

462
00:33:19,042 --> 00:33:21,544
and I'm not looking
to be department chief.

463
00:33:21,628 --> 00:33:25,465
Wow. Lot a confidence for a man
who once got disciplined for dozing.

464
00:33:27,884 --> 00:33:29,386
Sorry. That was…

465
00:33:32,180 --> 00:33:35,225
I can't help that I was
hired older than you.

466
00:33:38,019 --> 00:33:39,645
It just never stops, huh?

467
00:33:39,729 --> 00:33:40,981
Endless toil.

468
00:33:42,315 --> 00:33:43,441
Where's Mark?

469
00:33:43,525 --> 00:33:45,694
Sick or fired. Probably sick.

470
00:33:46,194 --> 00:33:48,279
They wouldn't have fired him
for my note thing, would they?

471
00:33:48,363 --> 00:33:51,163
No. No way. He did his stint
in the break room yesterday.

472
00:33:55,203 --> 00:33:56,913
MR. MILCHICK: <i>He said he was ill.</i>

473
00:33:56,997 --> 00:33:58,206
MS. COBEL: Did he sound ill?

474
00:33:58,290 --> 00:34:00,542
I don't know. He said it was abdominal.

475
00:34:02,085 --> 00:34:03,253
Funny timing.

476
00:34:04,921 --> 00:34:06,006
[SIGHS]

477
00:34:44,919 --> 00:34:48,003
DYLAN: The erasers are mostly
decorative, since we don't have pencils.

478
00:34:48,087 --> 00:34:50,423
Finger trap is fun, as long as
you know how to use it safely.

479
00:34:50,507 --> 00:34:54,219
But it's really more about what they
represent, how far you got in the file.

480
00:34:54,303 --> 00:34:57,764
-But why don't we always finish the files?
-'Cause they only keep so long.

481
00:34:57,848 --> 00:35:01,226
You know, we finish, on average,
one in five files before they expire.

482
00:35:01,310 --> 00:35:04,980
Which is better than it used to
be before Mark's freshman fluke.

483
00:35:05,064 --> 00:35:07,357
-[DISTORTED] What's a freshman fluke?
-[IRVING TYPING]

484
00:35:07,441 --> 00:35:09,443
[DYLAN SPEAKING INDISTINCTLY, DISTORTED]

485
00:35:16,992 --> 00:35:17,993
[SIGHS]

486
00:35:22,248 --> 00:35:24,749
[INDISTINCT SPEAKING CONTINUES, DISTORTED]

487
00:36:05,373 --> 00:36:06,457
[SCREAMS] No!

488
00:36:07,292 --> 00:36:08,293
Irv?

489
00:36:11,170 --> 00:36:12,505
I'm s… I'm sorry.

490
00:36:13,298 --> 00:36:14,348
MR. MILCHICK: Irving?

491
00:37:17,444 --> 00:37:20,694
We'll deduct the time you spent
dozing from your outie's paycheck.

492
00:37:21,031 --> 00:37:24,701
What will be harder to fix, Irving,
is my and Ms. Cobel's trust in you.

493
00:37:25,244 --> 00:37:28,121
IRVING: I'm so sorry, sir.
I'm just so sorry.

494
00:37:28,205 --> 00:37:31,166
Well, no one is hankering to
throw you in the break room.

495
00:37:31,875 --> 00:37:35,325
We'll do a wellness check with
Ms. Casey and go from there, all right?

496
00:37:35,462 --> 00:37:36,880
Thank you, Mr. Milchick.

497
00:37:52,478 --> 00:37:53,563
[SIGHS]

498
00:37:59,443 --> 00:38:00,695
Nothing scary yet.

499
00:38:09,745 --> 00:38:11,330
[GASPS] Oh, God! A four!

500
00:38:11,414 --> 00:38:12,582
Don't fuck around.

501
00:38:13,124 --> 00:38:16,377
I told you, you'll understand
when you see it, so just be patient.

502
00:38:17,003 --> 00:38:18,462
W-What even are these numbers?

503
00:38:18,546 --> 00:38:21,146
Like, do we even know what
we're supposedly cleaning?

504
00:38:23,301 --> 00:38:24,302
My theory?

505
00:38:26,012 --> 00:38:27,013
The sea.

506
00:38:29,307 --> 00:38:30,308
The sea?

507
00:38:31,017 --> 00:38:32,476
Yeah. Think about it.

508
00:38:32,560 --> 00:38:35,687
Okay, if our outies are up
there severing their brains,

509
00:38:35,771 --> 00:38:37,564
shit must have gotten pretty bad.

510
00:38:37,648 --> 00:38:40,317
Famine, plagues, et cetera.

511
00:38:40,401 --> 00:38:43,613
So what is a desperate humanity to do?

512
00:38:46,072 --> 00:38:47,073
Populate the sea?

513
00:38:47,157 --> 00:38:48,533
Populate the sea.

514
00:38:48,617 --> 00:38:52,370
But first, they gotta send probes
down to the sea to clean up

515
00:38:52,454 --> 00:38:55,916
all the deadly eels and shit,
'cause we can't cohabitate with that.

516
00:38:56,416 --> 00:38:59,919
So we send the probes down,
they send us the data coded,

517
00:39:00,003 --> 00:39:04,549
we sense what's eels, and then
we tell the probes what to blow up.

518
00:39:08,053 --> 00:39:09,403
This is the leading theory?

519
00:39:11,264 --> 00:39:14,351
Nah, Irv thinks we're cutting
swear words out of movies.

520
00:39:18,813 --> 00:39:22,413
But what is it? Like, what… what
are we actually workin' on down there?

521
00:39:24,486 --> 00:39:25,487
I don't know.

522
00:39:26,780 --> 00:39:29,630
I thought without severance it
would make more sense, but…

523
00:39:31,201 --> 00:39:33,870
They separate us from
the other departments.

524
00:39:33,954 --> 00:39:35,854
We don't even know how many there are.

525
00:39:40,293 --> 00:39:42,546
But I've been reintegrated
for two weeks now.

526
00:39:45,173 --> 00:39:46,925
I've been mapping out the floor.

527
00:39:58,644 --> 00:40:01,021
I hid the original for you when I left.

528
00:40:13,617 --> 00:40:16,662
[CRYING, PANTING]

529
00:40:21,000 --> 00:40:22,126
You okay?

530
00:40:30,259 --> 00:40:31,260
Sorry.

531
00:40:32,636 --> 00:40:33,929
Reintegration sickness.

532
00:40:36,557 --> 00:40:38,225
Never heard of that one.

533
00:40:39,018 --> 00:40:41,437
Because I'm the first
dipshit that's ever had it.

534
00:40:42,730 --> 00:40:46,567
Plus, living in a cold-ass greenhouse
doesn't help, but I can't go home.

535
00:40:49,695 --> 00:40:53,824
Okay, so what is it that happens
down there that's so bad?

536
00:40:57,703 --> 00:40:58,704
There's this room…

537
00:41:05,376 --> 00:41:07,795
We go in there when we don't act right.

538
00:41:15,553 --> 00:41:16,679
MARK: What is that?

539
00:41:23,394 --> 00:41:24,979
MR. MILCHICK: <i>I'm afraid you're not sorry.</i>

540
00:41:25,063 --> 00:41:30,317
MARK: [BREATHES HEAVILY] <i>Please.
I truly am. I'm sorry.</i>

541
00:41:30,401 --> 00:41:32,651
MR. MILCHICK: <i>Please
read the statement again.</i>

542
00:41:34,030 --> 00:41:38,450
MARK: <i>Forgive me for the
harm I have caused this world.</i>

543
00:41:38,534 --> 00:41:44,373
<i>None may atone for my actions but me,
and only in me shall their stain live on.</i>

544
00:41:44,457 --> 00:41:46,667
<i>I am thankful to have been caught,</i>

545
00:41:46,751 --> 00:41:50,838
<i>my fall cut short by
those with wizened hands.</i>

546
00:41:51,714 --> 00:41:55,343
<i>All I can be is sorry,
and that is all that I am.</i>

547
00:41:56,511 --> 00:42:00,931
MR. MILCHICK: <i>I'm afraid
you don't mean it. Again, please.</i>

548
00:42:01,015 --> 00:42:02,391
[MARK BREATHING HEAVILY]

549
00:42:02,475 --> 00:42:07,104
<i>Forgive me for the harm
I have caused this world.</i>

550
00:42:08,605 --> 00:42:11,900
<i>None may atone for my actions but me.</i>

551
00:42:15,362 --> 00:42:16,697
The fuck is that?

552
00:42:19,116 --> 00:42:20,367
That's the break room.

553
00:42:30,002 --> 00:42:31,587
[SIGHS]

554
00:42:39,887 --> 00:42:40,888
Hmm.

555
00:42:51,815 --> 00:42:52,816
[DOOR OPENS]

556
00:42:53,483 --> 00:42:56,069
-Oh! I'm… I'm sorry.
-Oh! No, I'm sorry, sir.

557
00:42:56,153 --> 00:42:58,363
I didn't think anybody was out here.

558
00:42:58,447 --> 00:43:00,865
I'm coming out from my session.

559
00:43:00,949 --> 00:43:06,622
I… I was just going in.
I was admiring the, uh, art while I wait.

560
00:43:07,497 --> 00:43:11,167
That piece hung in the
perpetuity wing for many years.

561
00:43:11,251 --> 00:43:14,128
I know. And it broke my
heart when they took it down.

562
00:43:14,712 --> 00:43:17,006
It's better here. It's calming.

563
00:43:20,218 --> 00:43:22,929
I'm Irving. Macrodata Refinement.

564
00:43:23,512 --> 00:43:24,972
Are you a department head?

565
00:43:25,056 --> 00:43:26,974
Well, Optics and Design.

566
00:43:27,725 --> 00:43:30,185
A two-person department, so, barely.

567
00:43:30,269 --> 00:43:32,396
Mmm. So this is your work, hmm?

568
00:43:32,480 --> 00:43:35,232
We don't paint them. We do hang them.

569
00:43:35,316 --> 00:43:40,445
I loved that you did the Ambrose cycle
in the team-building space last quarter.

570
00:43:40,529 --> 00:43:42,990
-Huh.
-I'd never seen it.

571
00:43:43,491 --> 00:43:45,993
Well, it's rare to meet a sophisticate.

572
00:43:46,911 --> 00:43:50,789
Most people only think of O&D
when new handbook totes come in.

573
00:43:50,873 --> 00:43:51,874
[BOTH CHUCKLE]

574
00:43:51,958 --> 00:43:53,750
Well, I love those too.

575
00:43:53,834 --> 00:43:55,210
-Yeah?
-Mm-hmm.

576
00:43:55,294 --> 00:43:57,672
There's new ones coming next month.

577
00:43:58,631 --> 00:44:00,424
Best design yet, in my view.

578
00:44:00,925 --> 00:44:05,471
Oh, wow! Well, that's all I'll
be thinking about until then.

579
00:44:06,097 --> 00:44:06,997
MS. CASEY: Irving?

580
00:44:24,698 --> 00:44:26,825
[BIRDSONG PLAYING]

581
00:44:31,621 --> 00:44:33,540
[AMBIENT MUSIC PLAYING]

582
00:44:38,837 --> 00:44:40,672
[BREATHES DEEPLY]

583
00:44:45,385 --> 00:44:46,678
All right, Irving.

584
00:44:47,512 --> 00:44:51,433
What I'd like to do is share with
you some facts about your outie.

585
00:44:53,018 --> 00:44:59,024
Because your outie is an exemplary person,
these facts should be very pleasing.

586
00:45:00,692 --> 00:45:04,696
Just relax your body
and be open to the facts.

587
00:45:05,488 --> 00:45:08,617
Try to enjoy each equally.

588
00:45:09,951 --> 00:45:13,163
These facts are not to be
shared outside this room.

589
00:45:14,706 --> 00:45:17,918
But for now, they're yours to enjoy.

590
00:45:23,423 --> 00:45:25,383
Your outie is generous.

591
00:45:28,011 --> 00:45:31,931
Your outie is fond of music
and owns many records.

592
00:45:33,682 --> 00:45:39,480
Your outie is a friend to children
and to the elderly and the insane.

593
00:45:41,523 --> 00:45:45,736
Your outie is strong and helped
someone lift a heavy object.

594
00:45:47,780 --> 00:45:53,452
Your outie attends many dances and
is popular among the other attendees.

595
00:45:54,787 --> 00:46:01,752
Your outie likes films and owns
a machine that can play them.

596
00:46:03,462 --> 00:46:08,926
Your outie is splendid and
can swim gracefully and well.

597
00:46:09,677 --> 00:46:11,637
-[CHUCKLES]
-I'm sorry.

598
00:46:12,513 --> 00:46:15,099
Please try to enjoy each fact equally,

599
00:46:15,766 --> 00:46:18,435
and not show preference
for any over the others.

600
00:46:19,019 --> 00:46:22,022
That's ten points off.
You have 90 points remaining.

601
00:46:22,106 --> 00:46:24,316
-Points?
-Please don't speak.

602
00:46:32,074 --> 00:46:34,660
Your outie won a game two weeks ago.

603
00:46:36,078 --> 00:46:38,663
Your outie values water.

604
00:46:39,956 --> 00:46:44,669
A photo of your outie with a
trophy was once in a newspaper.

605
00:46:46,296 --> 00:46:50,633
<i>Your outie has no fear
of muggers or knaves.</i>

606
00:46:52,594 --> 00:46:55,638
Your outie likes the sound of radar.

607
00:46:58,224 --> 00:47:03,104
Your outie is skilled at
kissing and lovemaking.

608
00:47:04,480 --> 00:47:05,856
Oh.

609
00:47:05,940 --> 00:47:06,941
I'm sorry.

610
00:47:07,025 --> 00:47:09,944
Please don't respond to any specific fact.

611
00:47:10,028 --> 00:47:11,821
That's ten points off.

612
00:47:11,905 --> 00:47:14,115
-I was just…
-Please don't speak further,

613
00:47:14,199 --> 00:47:18,036
or all remaining points will be deducted
and the wellness session will end.

614
00:47:35,803 --> 00:47:36,846
Okay.

615
00:47:57,783 --> 00:47:59,284
-Is it just the elevators?
-What?

616
00:47:59,368 --> 00:48:01,703
With the code detectors.
Or is it the stairwells too?

617
00:48:01,787 --> 00:48:03,705
Jesus. Stairwells too. Why?

618
00:48:03,789 --> 00:48:05,999
If no one accepts the resignations,

619
00:48:06,083 --> 00:48:07,876
what makes you think
they're being delivered at all?

620
00:48:07,960 --> 00:48:10,253
You gotta let the
info-smuggling notion go.

621
00:48:10,337 --> 00:48:11,588
Okay, but how good are the scanners?

622
00:48:11,672 --> 00:48:14,841
Like, what if you wrote the letters
funky, like one of those robot tests?

623
00:48:14,925 --> 00:48:16,801
-Hi, kids. What's for dinner?
-Nope!

624
00:48:16,885 --> 00:48:18,136
How was wellness?

625
00:48:18,220 --> 00:48:20,096
Great. Very restorative.

626
00:48:20,180 --> 00:48:21,973
I met the O&D department head.

627
00:48:22,057 --> 00:48:26,227
Burt? I've met that guy. He's a fuck.
You didn't tell him where we are, did you?

628
00:48:26,311 --> 00:48:28,396
Burt's not a fuck. And no.

629
00:48:28,480 --> 00:48:30,607
DYLAN: I'm asking for the
safety of this department.

630
00:48:30,691 --> 00:48:32,150
-I don't need to tell you…
-That's a myth.

631
00:48:32,234 --> 00:48:34,068
And don't pretend you
care about this department.

632
00:48:34,152 --> 00:48:35,778
You just want your waffle party.

633
00:48:35,862 --> 00:48:38,823
DYLAN: Oh, beg-fucking-pardon.
We're supposed to like the perks.

634
00:48:38,907 --> 00:48:42,911
"And all in Lumon's care shall revel
in the bounty of the incentives spur."

635
00:48:42,995 --> 00:48:45,705
Don't pervert a handbook
passage to me, okay?

636
00:48:45,789 --> 00:48:46,956
-Don't do it.
-Hey, guys?

637
00:48:47,040 --> 00:48:48,875
Lumon has been good
to us, and it is feckless to…

638
00:48:48,959 --> 00:48:49,960
Guys!

639
00:48:58,509 --> 00:48:59,760
That.

640
00:49:00,553 --> 00:49:01,554
DYLAN: Yep.

641
00:49:02,179 --> 00:49:03,722
Why is… Why is it, uh…

642
00:49:03,806 --> 00:49:06,517
You okay. Just fence off the
bad data like I showed you.

643
00:49:06,601 --> 00:49:08,477
Can you see the perimeter?

644
00:49:17,653 --> 00:49:19,071
DYLAN: And bin it.

645
00:49:31,292 --> 00:49:33,168
Boom. Fucking refined.

646
00:49:33,252 --> 00:49:36,088
-All right, Helly. Hip, hip.
-[BREATHES SHAKILY]

647
00:49:36,172 --> 00:49:39,175
They were scary. The numbers were scary.

648
00:49:46,766 --> 00:49:48,976
[CLOCK TICKING]

649
00:49:57,026 --> 00:50:00,403
MARK: Here's the couch.
I'll find you a sleeping bag.

650
00:50:01,279 --> 00:50:04,699
And you can use the shower if you want.

651
00:50:05,450 --> 00:50:06,868
What are you trying to say?

652
00:50:07,786 --> 00:50:09,120
[CHUCKLES]

653
00:50:09,204 --> 00:50:10,204
Um…

654
00:50:10,288 --> 00:50:13,166
[SIGHS] Thanks for doing this, Mark.

655
00:50:13,708 --> 00:50:16,544
Well, I, you know, figured
my work self would be pissed

656
00:50:16,628 --> 00:50:18,212
if I let you sleep in a greenhouse.

657
00:50:18,296 --> 00:50:20,715
[PETEY SIGHS, MUMBLING]

658
00:50:24,094 --> 00:50:25,345
Uh, light's on the left.

659
00:50:32,102 --> 00:50:33,144
[SIGHS]

660
00:50:53,039 --> 00:50:54,207
MARK: You okay in there?

661
00:50:54,291 --> 00:50:56,585
Yeah! Yeah, I'm good.

662
00:50:57,085 --> 00:50:58,252
[BREATHING HEAVILY]

663
00:50:58,336 --> 00:50:59,462
[CELL PHONE BUZZING]

664
00:51:06,886 --> 00:51:08,678
[SNIFFS, CHOKES]

665
00:51:08,762 --> 00:51:09,763
[SPLUTTERS]

666
00:51:10,639 --> 00:51:14,100
[GROANS, PANTING]

667
00:51:14,184 --> 00:51:15,311
[BUZZING CONTINUES]

668
00:51:16,645 --> 00:51:18,230
-[SNIFFS]
-[BUZZING STOPS]

669
00:51:22,484 --> 00:51:24,319
[SNEEZES]

670
00:51:24,403 --> 00:51:26,322
[PANTING]

671
00:51:35,581 --> 00:51:36,749
[GROANS]

672
00:51:39,335 --> 00:51:40,753
[PANTING]

673
00:51:57,353 --> 00:51:58,896
[GROANS]

674
00:52:09,573 --> 00:52:10,907
MARK: Petey, are you okay?

675
00:52:10,991 --> 00:52:12,785
["DAYDREAM IN BLUE" PLAYING]

